  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1000 PM CST Mon Nov 23 2026\n\nSmall chance for rain/snow Thanksgiving into Thursday night...\nAfter high pressure brings dry weather into Wednesday, pattern \nbecomes quite amplified Wednesday night into Thursday night. Main\nupper low looks to stay far enough south to keep primary precip\nchances Wednesday night into Thursday morning (cold enough for\nsnow) generally south of our area. As main system shifts east on\nThanksgiving, western fringe of widespread shield of mainly rain\ncould clip eastern WI. Chances for anything more than 0.05 inch of\nQPF have decreased. With it moving through later in the day into\nThursday evening, if it does occur, ptype would be mainly rain.\nOperational GFS still more aggressive in bringing precip in early\non Thursday, but is not supported by GFS ensembles or AIGFS model.\nECMWF idea, supported by EC ensembles and EC-AIFS remains farther\nsouth and keeps most precip south of WI most of the day on \nThanksgiving.\n\nBeyond this system, another weaker system brings small chances for\nrain/snow this weekend, though blend of ensembles and NBM suggest\nmain focus for precip this weekend will stay over Ohio Valley and\nnorth across Ontario Canada.   \n\nTemperatures: Coldest day over the next week will be Tuesday. After\na chilly start with lows maybe as low as single digits over part \nof northern WI early Tuesday morning, readings will push into the \nmid 30s. Shroud of mid and high clouds could keep these readings \neven lower if they arrive quick enough from the west. Conditions \nthen steadily warm rest of the week with highs back just above \nnormal on Wednesday (normal highs are mid 30s to 40), and into the\n40s Thanksgiving on into the weekend. Could even see some highs \nnear 50 degrees on Saturday ahead of a cold front that moves \nthrough later in the weekend.",
